1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,350
Hi there, my name is Connor,

2
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:06,250
and this is my demo for Applied Resonance,

3
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:10,760
which turns smart glasses into
an early warning system for machines.

4
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:15,640
Before you can notice that a machine sounds wrong,
you have to learn what normal sounds like.

5
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:18,600
Here, the real G2 glasses microphone
that I am wearing

6
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:22,440
listens to a normal pump for 30 seconds
and builds a baseline.

7
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:27,160
When playback changes to an abnormal recording
from the same pump at the same volume,

8
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:30,880
the change persists, and an alert
appears on the glasses.

9
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:34,960
That is where you see me tap the temple once,
which saves the previous 10 seconds

10
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:36,280
as evidence.

11
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:40,120
When the normal sound returns, however,
it goes back to listening.

12
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:45,400
That recovery is important because it shows
Applied Resonance can constantly update,

13
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:47,720
no matter what is going on.

14
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:54,400
I tested it on 9,755 industrial recordings
from DCASE, a standard research benchmark

15
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:57,000
for unusual machine sounds.

16
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,900
It reached an AUC of 0.693 on fans
and 0.880 on pumps,

17
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:10,640
above the published DCASE 2020
reference baselines.

18
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:15,560
Six months ago, I had never built anything
related to wearable hardware.

19
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:21,880
Now, however, I have a working loop from glasses
to phone, to model, and back to the display.

20
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:23,680
That is what I am proudest of.

21
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:28,000
I followed one question from my boxing gym
all the way to a real prototype.
